=== 3. what the runs say, line by line ===
  SGL4, SGL2      a single-member list keeps UBWC today (tail=0x2000 against a
                  0x200000 image), and the same-family read is EXACT: the fill by
                  copy and the integer sampler round-trip through a compressed
                  64-bit image.  That had not been measured before, and without it
                  the verdict below would mean nothing.
  NOL             the same content, no list, no UBWC, read both ways: EXACT.  The
                  views themselves are honest.
  DETECT          the fill given a row length in words instead of texels, on
                  purpose: 196352/262144 wrong, identical in all three runs.  The
                  rig sees wrong data, so "0 wrong" above and below is a statement.
  MIX42, MIX24    the pair in question.  With the knob off they are tiled without
                  UBWC and read EXACT both ways, as expected of uncompressed memory.
                  With the knob on the creates carry the metadata tail (0x202000) and
                  STILL read EXACT both ways - a compressed image created as 4x16
                  reads exactly through a 2x32 view, and the other way round, at
                  0/262144 wrong texels each.

=== 4. verdict ===
  On this A740 the two labels are one cell of the compressor, in the paths the probe
  exercises: a buffer->image copy in, and a compute texelFetch out.  Nothing in the
  whole test corpus regresses when the pair is treated as compatible, and nothing
  else in the driver changes behaviour - the knob moved two lines out of 621.

=== 5. what this does not show ===
  * It is not a claim that the chip compressed these images.  +0x2000 in memreq is an
    allocation of a metadata plane (journal 22.2).  What is measured is that the
    create took the UBWC decision and that the content still round-trips; whether the
    hardware wrote compressed bytes is a different detector (a fast-clear that leaves
    the bytes alone would be the direct one).
  * It says nothing about the 32-bit class, where the measured break is a channel
    order (journal 24: XV[BGRA8_as_RGBA8] MISMATCH, memory is swapped), nor about the
    8-bit class, which reads as zeros under UBWC (ADR-0007).  The pair measured here
    is one pair of labels of one class.
  * It does not license a rule keyed on the base format.  A R64_UINT view stays legal
    on an image created with STORAGE usage whatever this says (tu_formats.cc grants
    the feature under has_64b_image_atomics, journal 31), and a no-list image is
    viewable as every member of its class, so the class rule still has to answer for
    those members separately.
  * One geometry (512x512), one usage set without STORAGE, one reader engine.  Not
    mipmaps, not arrays, not multisample, not a r2d or d2r read, not the game.
